      <p class="pbs-eyebrow">Body Shape Guide</p>
      <h1 class="pbs-h1">Pear Body Shape</h1>
      <p class="pbs-subtitle">Hips visibly wider than the bust, with a waist that's present but not sharply carved — the second most common of the seven female body shapes, at roughly 20% of women. Here's the exact formula, how it differs from its closest cousin, and how to dress for it.</p>
    </header>
  </div>

  <div class="pbs-art">

    <div style="text-align:center;margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/assets/images/Pear-Body-Shape-Formula-Measurements-How-to-Dress-300x200.webp" alt="Pear body shape formula, measurements and how to dress" style="max-width:420px;width:100%;border-radius:16px;border:1px solid var(--pbs-border);" loading="lazy">
    </div>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-what">
      <h2>What Is a Pear Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>A pear body shape — also called triangle — has hips that are noticeably wider than the bust, without the sharp waist definition you'd see in an hourglass. It's the classic "narrower on top, fuller below" silhouette, and it's driven by where the frame naturally carries fullness: hips, thighs and seat rather than the bust or shoulders. If you haven't run your own numbers yet, our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> will tell you exactly which of the seven shapes you land in.</p>
      <p>The formula: hips have to be at least 3.6 inches wider than the bust, and the gap between hips and waist has to stay under 9 inches. That second condition is the part people miss — a pear isn't waistless, it just doesn't have the dramatic 9-plus-inch taper that defines an hourglass or a bottom hourglass. The waist is soft, not sharp.</p>
      <div class="pbs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> hips at least 3.6 inches wider than the bust, waist within 9 inches of the hip measurement. If your waist is more than 9 inches smaller than your hips, you're actually a bottom hourglass, not a pear — the styling advice shifts a little.</p>
      </div>
      <p>To see how a pear shape looks in three dimensions, tap the <strong>Pear</strong> preset in our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a> (female model, <em>Try a shape</em>) and switch between the front, side and back views. Or use Compare A/B to set it next to a bottom hourglass.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-rare">
      <h2>How Common Is the Pear Shape?</h2>
      <p>The most widely cited figures come from a 2006 NC State analysis of over 6,000 women, which puts the pear shape at roughly 20% of the population — the second most common shape after rectangle (~46%). Apple comes in around 14%, and the hourglass family, despite its cultural prominence, is the rarest at around 8%. These specific numbers trace to a press release rather than a peer-reviewed table, and later re-analyses using different methods have found different splits, so treat them as a general picture rather than an exact count.</p>
      <p>That makes pear one of the two shapes — alongside rectangle — that a majority of women are likely to recognise themselves in, which is exactly why so much mainstream styling content is really written for this shape even when it doesn't say so.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-compare">
      <h2>Pear vs Bottom Hourglass — What's the Real Difference?</h2>
      <p>These two are the pair most people mix up, because both have hips wider than the bust. The split comes down entirely to the waist.</p>
      <div class="pbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Pear (Triangle)</th><th scope="col">Bottom Hourglass</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Hips vs. bust</td><td>3.6 in or more wider</td><td>3.6–10 in wider</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist definition</td><td>Under 9 in smaller than hips (soft)</td><td>9 in or more smaller than hips (sharp)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual read</td><td>Gradual taper from hip to waist</td><td>A clear inward cut at the waist</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Add width up top to balance</td><td>Show off the waist, balance is secondary</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>You'll also see "spoon" used online as a related term — some calculators split out a spoon shape using a high-hip "shelf" measurement just below the waist. It's a genuine, well-documented distinction, but it's a subtler one than pear vs. bottom hourglass, and most people searching for spoon-shape advice find that the pear styling tips below work just as well for them. Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> flags this shelf pattern automatically when it shows up in your result.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-whr">
      <h2>Waist-to-Hip Ratio for a Pear Shape</h2>
      <p>Because fullness sits in the hips rather than the waist, pear shapes tend to land on the favourable side of waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) — typically somewhere around 0.70 to 0.80, under the WHO's 0.85 threshold for elevated risk in women. Fat carried in the hips and thighs (subcutaneous fat) behaves differently, metabolically, than fat carried around the waist and abdomen (visceral fat), which is part of why pear shapes are generally associated with lower cardiovascular risk than apple shapes at a similar body size.</p>
      <p>Run your exact numbers on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> rather than relying on the shape category alone — WHR uses your specific waist and hip figures, and it's worth checking on its own. For a fuller set — including bust-to-hip ratio and your body shape — try the <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for a Pear Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The styling goal is balance, not disguise: add visual width to the shoulders and upper body so the frame reads as proportioned top to bottom, rather than trying to minimise the hips.</p>

      <h3>Work and Formal</h3>
      <p>A structured blazer with a strong shoulder line, worn over a plain top, does most of the work on its own. Pencil skirts and straight trousers in darker, solid colours keep the lower half streamlined without clinging. A boat-neck or wide scoop-neck blouse widens the visual line across the collarbone.</p>

      <h3>Casual and Everyday</h3>
      <p>Bright colours, bold prints or textured fabric on top — paired with straight-leg or bootcut jeans in a dark, plain wash — is a formula that holds up in almost any casual setting. Save the pattern and detail for above the waist; keep the bottom simple.</p>

      <h3>Special Occasions</h3>
      <p>An A-line gown with an embellished, structured bodice does exactly what pear styling is built around: definition and detail up top, a clean line that skims (rather than clings to) the hips below. Off-the-shoulder necklines are another strong option for the same reason.</p>

      <h3>Swimwear</h3>
      <p>A top with embellishment, a high neck, or a bit of padding — paired with a plain, darker bottom in a boyshort or high-waisted cut — balances the frame well. Avoid a plain triangle top over a bold or light-coloured bottom, which does the opposite of what you want.</p>

      <h3>What to Avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Skinny jeans or tapered trousers in light washes, which draw the eye straight to the widest point</li>
        <li>Clingy, hip-hugging fabric on the lower half with no structure on top to balance it</li>
        <li>Plain, unadorned tops when the bottom half already has pattern or shine — it tips the balance further down instead of up</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-measure">
      <h2>Common Pear Shape Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The formula holds across every size range — it's the relationship between the numbers that matters, not their absolute size:</p>
      <div class="pbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Frame</th><th scope="col">Bust</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Hips</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Petite</td><td>33 in</td><td>32 in</td><td>39 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average</td><td>37 in</td><td>36 in</td><td>44 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plus-size</td><td>44 in</td><td>43 in</td><td>51 in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Notice the waist sits close to the bust in every row — the defining gap is between hips and everything else, not a dramatic waist taper. That's the detail that separates a pear from a bottom hourglass at the same hip measurement.</p>
      <p>Try the average row in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">Body Visualizer</a>: switch to inches, enter a 37 in bust, 36 in waist and 44 in hips, and the live analysis confirms the shape while you rotate the model.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pbs-sec" id="pbs-faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>What is the exact formula for a pear body shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Hips at least 3.6 inches wider than the bust, and a hip-to-waist gap of under 9 inches. Both parts matter — the first rules out rectangle and hourglass shapes, and the second is what separates pear from bottom hourglass, which has the same wider-hip pattern but a much more defined waist.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Is pear the same as spoon body shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>They're related but not identical. Spoon is a more specific pattern where there's a pronounced "shelf" at the high hip, just below the waist, measured separately from the full hip. Pear is the broader category. If a calculator gives you spoon specifically, pear styling advice still applies — the shelf mainly affects fit at the high-hip area of trousers and skirts.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Pear vs bottom hourglass — which one am I?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Check the gap between your hips and your waist. Under 9 inches, you're a pear. Nine inches or more, you're a bottom hourglass. Both have hips wider than the bust — the waist definition is the entire difference.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Is a pear shape healthier than an apple shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>In terms of fat-distribution risk specifically, research generally does favour pear over apple — hip and thigh fat behaves differently, metabolically, than abdominal fat, and is linked to lower cardiovascular risk at a comparable body size. That's a population-level pattern, not an individual guarantee; overall health depends on far more than shape alone.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>Can a pear shape change with weight loss?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Weight loss reduces fat everywhere to some degree, but it tends to make an existing shape more pronounced rather than converting it into a different one — hip and thigh fat is often the last to reduce for pear-shaped bodies specifically. Strength training that builds the shoulders and upper back is a more direct way to shift the visual proportion.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="pbs-faq"><summary>What's the most flattering swimsuit for a pear shape?</summary><div class="pbs-faq-body">
        <p>Tops with detail, structure or a high neckline paired with a plain, darker bottom in boyshort or high-waisted styles. The goal is the same as with everyday clothing: add visual interest above the waist so the frame reads as balanced rather than bottom-heavy.</p>
      </div></details>
    </section>

    <p>Most "pear body shape" advice online skips the waist-to-hip distinction entirely and treats every wider-hipped body the same way — which is how pear and bottom hourglass end up with identical styling tips despite needing slightly different approaches. Once you know which side of that 9-inch line you're on, the advice gets a lot more specific.</p>

